\chapter{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
Compile-Time Regex
Engine}{The  Compile-Time Regex Engine}}\label{ch9-the-pattern-compile-time-regex-engine}

Chapter 8 introduced the \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} /
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}when} /
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}otherwise} dispatch machinery and
showed that a
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} clause
can carry a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
key in addition to the more conventional value-based keys. That chapter
treated the pattern key as an opaque token: something with a
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member that the dispatch
table invokes behind the scenes. This chapter opens the box. It
documents the
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
template in full, explains the matching algorithm it implements, and
shows the precise regex subset it supports.

\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
a compile-time, regex-like matcher intended for short classification
tasks: distinguishing an integer literal from an identifier, recognising
a keyword, or routing a token kind. It is not a general
regular-expression engine. It deliberately implements only a small,
well-defined subset of regex syntax, just enough to express the
character classes and quantifiers that show up in lexical
micro-grammars. For richer recognition the library provides the PEG
engine described in Chapters 27 and 28, which supports sequencing,
alternation, and recursive grammars. This chapter is concerned solely
with the lightweight
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
facility.

The design goal is to keep the matcher small enough to live entirely in
a header, to be \texttt{constexpr}-evaluable, and to require no
allocation. Every match runs against
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} inputs
and produces a \texttt{bool}. There is no capture, no grouping, and no
state carried between calls. The matcher is a pure function of two
strings: the pattern, baked into the type at compile time, and the
input, supplied at the call site.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
Template}{The  Template}}\label{ch9-the-patterns-template}

\texttt{pattern} is a class template parameterised by a non-type
template parameter of type
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}FixedString}. The
\texttt{FixedString} NTTP (Chapter 4) is what makes it possible to write
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}"[0-9]+"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
directly at the source level: the string literal is converted into a
compile-time constant that can be carried as a template argument. The
pattern text is therefore fixed for the lifetime of the type. Two
distinct pattern strings denote two distinct types, and the compiler is
free to instantiate each exactly once.

The public surface of the template is intentionally tiny. It exposes two
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}} members: a \texttt{value} of type
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} that
mirrors the pattern text, and a
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} function that tests whether
a given input satisfies the pattern. Everything else is private. The
following block shows the template definition in the form a user would
reproduce in a manual page.

\begin{code}
namespace dsl {

template <FixedString S> struct pattern
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();

  static constexpr bool matches (std::string_view input)
  {
    return match_here (S.view (), input);
  }

  // ... private helpers ...
};

} // namespace dsl
\end{code}

The \texttt{value} member is provided as a convenience for diagnostics,
logging, and table construction. It is the textual form of the pattern,
recovered from the \texttt{FixedString} NTTP via
\texttt{S.\allowbreak{}view(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. Because it is
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}}, taking its address or binding it
to a \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} parameter costs nothing at run
time. The \texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member is the
operational interface. It delegates to the private recursive matcher
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}here}, passing both the pattern text and the
input. The recursion is the heart of the engine and is examined in
detail below.

Because both members are \texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}}, the
matcher can be used in \texttt{if constexpr} branches, in
\texttt{consteval} functions, and in any other context that demands a
compile-time result. The example shipped as
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}04-pattern-class\allowbreak{}es.\allowbreak{}cpp}
uses it from a runtime lambda, but the same calls are equally valid
inside a \texttt{constexpr} context. The matcher performs no heap
allocation, no threading, and no I/O; it is a pure function over two
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} ranges.

\section{Anchored Full-Match
Semantics}\label{ch9-anchored-full-match-semantics}

The most important semantic fact about
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
is that the match is anchored at both ends. A pattern matches an input
if and only if the entire input, from the first character to the last,
is described by the pattern. There is no notion of a partial or
substring match. This is the same convention used by
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}regex\_\allowbreak{}match} rather
than \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}regex\_\allowbreak{}search}.
Users coming from ECMAScript regex semantics, where matches default to
searching, should note the difference.

The anchoring has two practical consequences. First, a pattern such as
\texttt{"[0-9]+"} does not match the input \texttt{"abc123"}; it matches
\texttt{"123"} but not a string that merely contains \texttt{"123"}.
Second, the engine does not need a \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} or
\texttt{\$} anchor to express full matching, because full matching is
the default. The characters \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$}
are accepted by the parser but are silently ignored: they are
convenience tokens for users who like to write anchors explicitly, not
operational directives.

This anchoring decision is what makes
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
usable as a classification predicate in a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table. A
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}"[0-9]+"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
clause asserts that the \emph{entire} key matches the integer pattern,
not merely that the key contains an integer somewhere. That gives
dispatch a precise, total-predicate semantics: for any given input key,
at most one pattern in a well-formed table will match, provided the
patterns are mutually exclusive. The engine itself does not enforce
mutual exclusivity, but the anchoring makes it straightforward to
achieve by construction.

The decision also keeps the matcher small: it starts at offset zero
rather than searching every input position. Simple patterns are
inexpensive, but multiple overlapping repetitions can require
combinatorial backtracking. Use \texttt{StaticRegex} for larger
regular-expression workloads.

\section{Supported Syntax Subset}\label{ch9-supported-syntax-subset}

The engine recognises a deliberately small grammar. The supported tokens
are: the wildcard \texttt{.\allowbreak{}}, which matches any single
character; a character class \texttt{[abc]}, which matches any one of
the listed characters; a range \texttt{[a-z]}, which appears inside a
class and matches any character in the inclusive range; the postfix
quantifier \texttt{*}, meaning zero or more of the preceding atom; and
the postfix quantifier \texttt{+}, meaning one or more of the preceding
atom. The anchors \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} are parsed
and discarded.

Each of these tokens is documented in the comment block attached to the
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member, which is reproduced
here for reference.

\begin{code}
  /**
   * @brief Minimal regex-like match: anchored prefix/suffix, literal chars,
   * [class], *, +.
   *
   * Supported syntax (subset):
   *   .       any character
   *   [abc]   character class
   *   [a-z]   range in class
   *   *       zero or more of previous
   *   +       one or more of previous
   *   ^       (ignored, match is always anchored at start)
   *   $       (ignored, match is always anchored at end)
   *
   * @param input String to test.
   * @return true if the entire input matches the pattern.
   */
\end{code}

Any character that is not one of these special tokens is treated as a
literal. The literal \texttt{'a'} matches the character \texttt{'a'} and
nothing else. Literals are case-sensitive; the engine has no
case-insensitive mode. Because there is no escape mechanism, the
characters \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} \texttt{[} \texttt{]} \texttt{*}
\texttt{+} \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} \texttt{\$} \texttt{-} cannot be
matched as literals when they appear in a position where the parser
would interpret them as operators. This is one of the trade-offs of
keeping the grammar small; users who need to match these characters
literally should use the PEG engine (Chapter 27) instead.

The quantifiers \texttt{*} and \texttt{+} apply to the immediately
preceding atom. An atom is either a single literal character, the
\texttt{.\allowbreak{}} wildcard, or a complete
\texttt{[.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]} class. Quantifiers
do not compose: \texttt{[a-z]*+} is not meaningful and is not supported.
There is no \texttt{?} quantifier, no \texttt{\{n,\allowbreak{}m\}}
counted repetition, and no grouping construct that would allow a
quantifier to apply to a sub-expression of more than one atom.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}class}
Helper}{The  Helper}}\label{ch9-the-match_class-helper}

Character classes are handled by a dedicated helper,
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}class}. The helper takes two arguments: the
body of the class (the text between the brackets, with the brackets
themselves stripped), and a single candidate character. It returns
\texttt{true} if the candidate matches any element of the class, and
\texttt{false} otherwise. The body is scanned left to right, and the
function returns on the first match.

The scan alternates between two interpretations of the body. At each
position it checks whether the next three characters form a range, that
is, whether the character two positions ahead is a hyphen. If so, the
candidate is tested against the inclusive range from the current
character to the character after the hyphen, and the scan advances by
three. Otherwise the current character is treated as a singleton and the
scan advances by one. The following definition is the verbatim form
found in the header.

\begin{code}
  static constexpr bool
  match_class (std::string_view cls, char c)
  {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cls.size ();)
      {
        if (i + 2 < cls.size () && cls[i + 1] == '-')
          {
            if (c >= cls[i] && c <= cls[i + 2])
              return true;
            i += 3;
          }
        else
          {
            if (cls[i] == c)
              return true;
            ++i;
          }
      }
    return false;
  }
\end{code}

A range is recognised by the presence of a hyphen at offset
\texttt{i + 1}, with a character available at offset \texttt{i + 2}. The
check
\texttt{i + 2 \textless{}\allowbreak{} cls.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
guards against a trailing hyphen at the end of the class body; in that
case the hyphen is treated as a literal singleton because the range form
is not available. This is why a pattern such as \texttt{[-a]} matches
either a hyphen or the letter \texttt{a}, and why \texttt{[a-]} matches
either an \texttt{a} or a hyphen. The behaviour falls out of the index
test, not from a special case.

Classes may mix singletons and ranges freely. The pattern
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}]} matches any ASCII letter, upper or
lower case, or an underscore. The pattern \texttt{[0-9.\allowbreak{}]}
matches a digit or a full stop; here the \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} inside
a class is a literal, because \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}class} does
not interpret it as a wildcard. Inside a class, every non-hyphen
character is literal. This is a useful rule to remember: the wildcard
meaning of \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} applies only outside classes.

The class engine has no notion of negation. There is no
\texttt{[\textasciicircum{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]}
form. A class always asserts membership, never exclusion. Users who need
to recognise ``anything except a digit'' must phrase the pattern
differently, typically by listing the permitted characters explicitly or
by structuring the surrounding
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table so that an earlier
clause consumes the digit case and a later clause handles the remainder.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one}
Helper}{The  Helper}}\label{ch9-the-match_one-helper}

Once the matcher has decided which atom is next in the pattern, it needs
a uniform way to test whether a given input character satisfies that
atom. That is the role of \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one}. The helper
takes three arguments: a \texttt{char} indicating the kind of atom
(\texttt{'['} for a class, \texttt{'.\allowbreak{}'} for the wildcard,
or any other character for a literal), the class body \texttt{cls} (used
only when the first argument is \texttt{'['}), and the candidate
character \texttt{c}. It returns \texttt{true} if the candidate
satisfies the atom.

\begin{code}
  static constexpr bool
  match_one (char pat, std::string_view cls, char c)
  {
    if (pat == '[')
      return match_class (cls, c);
    if (pat == '.')
      return true;
    return pat == c;
  }
\end{code}

The three cases are mutually exclusive and are tested in order. A class
atom delegates to \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}class} with the
precomputed body. The wildcard atom unconditionally returns
\texttt{true}, because \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} matches any character,
including newline or NUL should either appear in the input. A literal
atom performs a single character comparison. The helper is small enough
to be inlined trivially, and the compiler typically folds the dispatch
into the surrounding recursion.

The reason \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one} takes both the atom kind and
the class body as separate arguments, rather than taking the pattern
substring directly, is that the surrounding
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}here} has already parsed the class once and
stored the body in a local \texttt{cls} variable. Passing the body
forward avoids re-parsing the class on every recursive call. This is a
small but real efficiency, and it keeps the recursion shallow in terms
of work per frame.

The atom-kind parameter uses \texttt{'['} as a sentinel for ``this is a
class''. This is safe because a literal \texttt{'['} cannot otherwise
reach \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one}: the parser in
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}here} intercepts \texttt{'['} and rewrites
it into the class path before the atom is tested. Similarly,
\texttt{'.\allowbreak{}'} is intercepted only in the wildcard sense; a
literal dot would require an escape, which the engine does not provide.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}here}
Recursion}{The  Recursion}}\label{ch9-the-match_here-recursion}

The core of the engine is \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}here}. It takes
two \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
arguments: the remaining pattern and the remaining input. It returns
\texttt{true} if the remaining input is fully consumed by the remaining
pattern. The function is recursive, with one recursive call per atom in
the pattern, plus additional recursive calls in the quantifier branches.
The verbatim definition is reproduced below; the remainder of this
section walks through it clause by clause.

\begin{code}
  static constexpr bool
  match_here (std::string_view pat, std::string_view str)
  {
    if (pat.empty ())
      return str.empty ();
    if (pat[0] == '^')
      return match_here (pat.substr (1), str);
    if (pat == "$")
      return str.empty ();

    // Parse class [...]
    std::string_view cls;
    std::size_t pat_advance = 1;
    char first = pat[0];
    if (first == '[')
      {
        auto end = pat.find (']', 1);
        if (end == std::string_view::npos)
          return false;
        cls = pat.substr (1, end - 1);
        pat_advance = end + 1;
      }

    // Check for quantifier
    char quant = 0;
    if (pat_advance < pat.size ()
        && (pat[pat_advance] == '*' || pat[pat_advance] == '+'))
      quant = pat[pat_advance++];

    auto rest = pat.substr (pat_advance);

    if (quant == '*')
      {
        // Try matching zero occurrences first, then one or more
        if (match_here (rest, str))
          return true;
        while (!str.empty () && match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
          {
            str.remove_prefix (1);
            if (match_here (rest, str))
              return true;
          }
        return false;
      }
    if (quant == '+')
      {
        // One or more
        if (str.empty () || !match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
          return false;
        str.remove_prefix (1);
        if (match_here (rest, str))
          return true;
        while (!str.empty () && match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
          {
            str.remove_prefix (1);
            if (match_here (rest, str))
              return true;
          }
        return false;
      }

    // Exactly one
    if (str.empty () || !match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
      return false;
    return match_here (rest, str.substr (1));
  }
\end{code}

The function begins with the empty-pattern case, which requires empty
input. A leading \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} is discarded because
matching already starts at offset zero. A remaining pattern consisting
only of \texttt{\$} also requires empty input, so a trailing end anchor
never consumes a literal dollar sign.

After the base cases, the function parses the current atom. It records
the first character of the pattern in \texttt{first} and sets
\texttt{pat\_\allowbreak{}advance} to one, the default advance for a
single-character atom. If that first character is \texttt{[}, it
searches for the matching \texttt{]} starting at position one. If no
\texttt{]} is found the pattern is malformed and the function returns
\texttt{false}. Otherwise the class body is captured into \texttt{cls}
and \texttt{pat\_\allowbreak{}advance} is advanced to the position just
past the \texttt{]}. This parsing happens once per atom, not once per
character, which is the efficiency motivation mentioned earlier.

With the atom parsed, the function checks for a trailing quantifier. It
looks at the character immediately after the atom, which sits at index
\texttt{pat\_\allowbreak{}advance}. If that character is \texttt{*} or
\texttt{+}, it is recorded in \texttt{quant} and
\texttt{pat\_\allowbreak{}advance} is advanced past it. The variable
\texttt{rest} is then bound to the pattern suffix that follows the atom
(and its quantifier, if any). At this point the function has decomposed
the pattern into three parts: the current atom (\texttt{first},
optionally with \texttt{cls}), an optional quantifier (\texttt{quant}),
and the rest of the pattern (\texttt{rest}).

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{*}
Branch}{The  Branch}}\label{ch9-the-branch}

The \texttt{*} quantifier means ``zero or more of the preceding atom''.
The implementation first tries the zero case: it recurses on
\texttt{rest} with the input unchanged. If that succeeds, the match is
done and the function returns \texttt{true} immediately. This is the
backtrack-friendly way of expressing that the atom is allowed to match
nothing.

If the zero case fails, the function enters a \texttt{while} loop that
consumes input characters one at a time, provided each satisfies the
atom via \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one}. After each successful
consumption it recurses on \texttt{rest} with the shortened input. As
soon as a recursion succeeds, the function returns \texttt{true}. If the
loop exhausts either the input or the matching characters without a
successful recursion, the function returns \texttt{false}.

This loop tests possible repetition lengths in ascending order. If
consuming three characters permits the suffix to match, it succeeds
without requiring a four-character repetition. Since the API returns
only a Boolean and has no captures, this search order does not change
the recognized language.

The loop may retry a suffix many times. Multiple overlapping quantified
atoms can multiply those retries; there is no general quadratic bound.
Keep this matcher for small classification patterns and use the
NFA-based \texttt{StaticRegex} for larger inputs.

The order of the two strategies matters. Trying the zero case first
means that an empty match is preferred whenever it allows the rest of
the pattern to succeed. For a pattern such as \texttt{[a-z]*x} against
the input \texttt{"x"}, the engine first tries matching \texttt{[a-z]*}
against the empty prefix, which leaves \texttt{x} to match the literal
\texttt{x}, and succeeds immediately. Trying the greedy case first would
have consumed the \texttt{x} and then failed to match the trailing
literal, requiring a backtrack. The zero-first ordering avoids that
backtrack in this common case.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{+}
Branch}{The  Branch}}\label{ch9-the-branch-1}

The \texttt{+} quantifier means ``one or more of the preceding atom''.
Its implementation is similar to the \texttt{*} branch but with the zero
case removed. The function first requires that the input is non-empty
and that its first character satisfies the atom; if not, the match fails
immediately. This is the difference between \texttt{+} and \texttt{*}:
\texttt{+} demands at least one match, \texttt{*} permits none.

After consuming the mandatory first character, the function tests the
suffix immediately. If needed, it consumes more matching characters one
at a time and retries the suffix after each. This gives \texttt{+} the
same backtracking completeness as \texttt{*}, with a minimum repetition
count of one.

For example, \texttt{[0-9]+9} matches \texttt{"99"}: the repetition
consumes the first nine and the suffix consumes the second.
\texttt{[a-z]+z} similarly matches \texttt{"az"}. Both C++ and D
recognize these cases, and the regression suite checks them.

\section{The Exactly-One Fallthrough}\label{ch9-the-exactly-one-fallthrough}

If no quantifier is present, the function falls through to the
exactly-one case. This is the simplest path. It requires that the input
is non-empty and that its first character satisfies the atom via
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one}. If so, it recurses on \texttt{rest}
with the input shortened by one character. If not, it returns
\texttt{false}.

This is the path taken by literal characters, by \texttt{.\allowbreak{}}
wildcards without a quantifier, and by character classes without a
quantifier. The recursion advances the pattern by one atom and the input
by one character, maintaining the invariant that the remaining input
must be exactly what the remaining pattern describes. The base case at
the top of the function --- empty pattern implies empty input ---
terminates the recursion when all atoms have been consumed.

Because each recursive call in this branch advances the pattern by
exactly one atom, the depth of recursion is bounded by the number of
atoms in the pattern. For a pattern of length N the recursion depth is
at most N. This makes the exactly-one path predictable and cheap, and it
is the path that the overwhelming majority of atoms in a typical pattern
take.

The trailing \texttt{\$} is handled by the earlier end-anchor base case.
Thus \texttt{[0-9]+\$} and \texttt{[0-9]+} recognize the same
whole-input strings.

\section{Repetition Search and
Backtracking}\label{ch9-repetition-search-and-backtracking}

Both repetition operators test the suffix at every admissible repetition
length. The C++ implementation searches shorter lengths first; D
searches longer lengths first. With whole-input Boolean matching and no
captures, these strategies produce the same result.

For most classification patterns, the rest of the pattern is empty or a
fixed literal, and the distinction is invisible. The interesting cases
arise when a quantifier is followed by another atom that overlaps the
quantified class. Consider the pattern \texttt{[a-z]*z} against the
input \texttt{"abz"}. The \texttt{*} branch first tries the zero case:
matching \texttt{z} against \texttt{"abz"} fails, because the first
character is \texttt{a}. The loop then consumes \texttt{a}, retries
\texttt{z} against \texttt{"bz"} (fails), consumes \texttt{b}, retries
\texttt{z} against \texttt{"z"} (succeeds), and returns \texttt{true}.
The engine finds the split because of the per-consumption recursion in
the \texttt{*} branch.

The same pattern against \texttt{"abzc"} fails because no split leaves
exactly the suffix \texttt{"z"}. A candidate that leaves \texttt{"zc"}
also fails: whole-input matching requires consuming the final
\texttt{c}.

The takeaway for users is that
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
implements a small, predictable subset of regex backtracking. It is
sufficient for the lexical-classification tasks it was designed for, and
it behaves consistently across all inputs. Where richer semantics are
required --- non-greedy quantifiers, alternation, grouping --- the PEG
engine in Chapters 27 and 28 is the appropriate tool.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Integration with
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match}}{Integration with  and }}\label{ch9-integration-with-when-and-dslmatch}

The \texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
template is designed to be used as the key of a
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} clause.
Chapter 8 covered the consumer side in detail; this section covers the
producer side, that is, what
\texttt{WhenClause:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}try\_\allowbreak{}invoke}
does when it encounters a pattern key. The relevant code is the
\texttt{requires} check at the top of \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}invoke}.

\begin{code}
    using KeyT = std::remove_cvref_t<decltype (Key)>;
    if constexpr (requires { KeyT::matches (std::string_view{}); })
      {
        if constexpr (std::convertible_to<K, std::string_view>)
          {
            if (KeyT::matches (std::string_view (key)))
              {
                out = handler (key, std::forward<Args> (args)...);
                return true;
              }
          }
      }
    else if constexpr (std::equality_comparable_with<K, decltype (Key)>)
      {
        if (key == Key)
          {
            out = handler (key, std::forward<Args> (args)...);
            return true;
          }
      }
\end{code}

The detection is structural rather than nominal.
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}invoke} does not ask whether \texttt{KeyT} is
a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiation; it asks whether \texttt{KeyT} exposes a
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view)\allowbreak{}}
member. Any type that satisfies this requirement is treated as a pattern
key. This is a deliberate design choice: it allows users to plug in
their own matcher types, provided they expose the same
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} bool matches(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view)\allowbreak{}}
shape. The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
template is the canonical such type, but it is not the only one the
dispatch machinery will accept.

When the \texttt{requires} clause is satisfied,
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}invoke} further requires that the runtime key
be convertible to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}. This
is what makes pattern-based dispatch usable with \texttt{const char*},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string},
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}, and
any other string-like type. The key is converted to a
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} and passed to
\texttt{KeyT:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
If the match succeeds, the handler is invoked with the original key (not
the converted view) and any trailing arguments, and the clause returns
\texttt{true}. If the match fails, the clause returns \texttt{false} and
the dispatch table proceeds to the next clause.

This routing is what allows a single
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table to mix value-based
and pattern-based clauses freely. Value-based clauses use the
\texttt{else if} branch with
\texttt{equality\_\allowbreak{}comparable\_\allowbreak{}with};
pattern-based clauses use the \texttt{if} branch with \texttt{requires}.
The two are mutually exclusive at compile time: a key type either has a
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member or it does not.
There is no ambiguity, and there is no run-time cost to the dispatch,
because the \texttt{if constexpr} discards the unused branch at compile
time.

\section{A First Example: Classifying Integers and
Words}\label{ch9-a-first-example-classifying-integers-and-words}

The simplest use of
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
direct invocation of \texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} from a
lambda or function. The example shipped as
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}04-pattern-class\allowbreak{}es.\allowbreak{}cpp}
shows the canonical pattern: a small lambda that tests an input against
several patterns in sequence and returns a label.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto classify = [](std::string_view s) {
    if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(s)) return "int";
    if (dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">::matches(s)) return "word";
    return "other";
  };
  std::cout << classify("123") << " " << classify("abc") << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The patterns here are mutually exclusive by construction. A string of
digits satisfies \texttt{[0-9]+} and not \texttt{[a-z]+}. A string of
lowercase letters satisfies \texttt{[a-z]+} and not \texttt{[0-9]+}. A
string containing any other character, such as \texttt{"a1"} or
\texttt{"@x"}, satisfies neither and falls through to the
\texttt{"other"} return. Because matching is anchored, \texttt{"a1"}
does not match \texttt{[a-z]+} even though it begins with letters; the
entire input must be described by the pattern.

The order of the tests matters. A string such as \texttt{"123"} matches
the first pattern and short-circuits; the second test is never run.
Reordering the tests would change the result for inputs that could match
more than one pattern, although in this particular example the patterns
are disjoint. In general, when patterns overlap, the first matching
clause wins, exactly as in a \texttt{switch} statement with a
\texttt{default}.

This style of classification is appropriate when the set of categories
is small and fixed. For larger or more dynamic taxonomies, embedding the
patterns in a \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table is
preferable, because it lets the dispatch machinery handle the ordering
and fallthrough uniformly. That style is shown below in the discussion
of example 19.

\section{Identifiers and Keywords}\label{ch9-identifiers-and-keywords}

A more realistic lexical classification distinguishes identifiers,
keywords, and numbers. Identifiers in most C-like languages match the
pattern \texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*}: an
initial letter or underscore, followed by zero or more letters,
underscores, or digits. Keywords are a fixed set of literal strings.
Numbers match \texttt{[0-9]+}. The example shipped as
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}15-tokenize-pars\allowbreak{}e-integration.\allowbreak{}cpp}
combines all three.

\begin{code}
  auto classify_by_key = [](std::string_view token) {
    if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches (token))
      return LexemeClass::Number;
    if (dsl::pattern<"if">::matches (token)
        || dsl::pattern<"for">::matches (token)
        || dsl::pattern<"while">::matches (token))
      return LexemeClass::Keyword;
    if (dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches (token))
      return LexemeClass::Identifier;
    return LexemeClass::Other;
  };
\end{code}

The number test comes first because it is the cheapest and the most
specific. The keyword test comes second and consists of three literal
patterns combined by \texttt{||}. Each literal pattern is a
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiation with a fixed string;
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns \texttt{true} only
when the input is exactly that string, because of the end-anchoring. The
identifier test comes last and is the most general; it would also match
the keywords, which is why the keyword test must precede it.

The identifier pattern is a good illustration of how atoms and
quantifiers compose in this engine. The first atom is the class
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}]}, which matches one letter or
underscore. The second atom is the class
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]} followed by the \texttt{*}
quantifier, which matches zero or more word characters. The engine
parses this as two atoms: a single class, and a class-with-quantifier.
There is no grouping construct, but none is needed, because the
quantifier already applies to the immediately preceding atom, which is
exactly the second class.

The keyword test uses three separate
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiations rather than a single pattern with alternation. This is
because the engine does not support the \texttt{|} operator. Users who
need alternation within a single pattern must either enumerate the
alternatives as separate
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiations combined by \texttt{||}, as shown here, or move to the
PEG engine. For small fixed keyword sets the enumeration approach is
clear and efficient, and it has the added benefit of making each keyword
a distinct type, which can be useful for diagnostics.

\section{Tokenizer-Kind
Classification}\label{ch9-tokenizer-kind-classification}

The example shipped as
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}19-pattern-match\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}cpp}
shows the same classification idea in an even more compact form,
returning string labels rather than enum values.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto token_kind = [](std::string_view t) {
    if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(t)) return "INT";
    if (dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">::matches(t)) return "ID";
    return "UNK";
  };
  std::cout << token_kind("abc") << " " << token_kind("99") << "\n";
}
\end{code}

This example is deliberately minimal. It shows that
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
usable as a one-liner predicate, with no setup, no state, and no
dependencies beyond the header itself. The two patterns are disjoint and
the fallthrough case returns a generic label. The example is a useful
starting point for users who want to experiment with the matcher before
integrating it into a larger dispatch table.

The simplicity of this example also highlights a limitation. Because the
engine does not support \texttt{\textbackslash{}d} or other shorthand
classes, every classification pattern must spell out its character
ranges explicitly. A pattern such as \texttt{[a-z]+} will not match
uppercase letters, digits, or underscores; if those should be classified
as identifiers, the pattern must be widened, as in the
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*} form used in
the previous example. Choosing the right pattern is a matter of
understanding the lexical rules of the language being classified.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Patterns Inside a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match}
Table}{Patterns Inside a  Table}}\label{ch9-patterns-inside-a-dslmatch-table}

The most powerful use of
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
as a key inside a \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table.
In this style, the dispatch machinery itself runs the pattern tests, and
the user supplies only the handlers. The example from
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}15-tokenize-pars\allowbreak{}e-integration.\allowbreak{}cpp}
continues by defining a dispatch table over the \texttt{LexemeClass}
enum, where the classification has already been performed by the lambda
shown above. The table itself uses value-based clauses for the enum, but
the same machinery supports pattern-based clauses directly.

\begin{code}
  auto dispatch = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Number> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":number(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Keyword> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":keyword(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Identifier> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":identifier(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":other(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }));
\end{code}

In this example the pattern-based classification and the value-based
dispatch are kept separate, with the classification lambda producing an
enum that the table then consumes. This separation is a matter of style.
A more compact design would place the patterns directly in the table, as
in the following sketch, which the \texttt{requires}-based detection in
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}invoke} fully supports.

\begin{code}
  auto dispatch = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">> (
          [] (std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":number(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">> (
          [] (std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":identifier(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":other(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }));
\end{code}

Here the table is invoked directly with the token string. The first
clause whose pattern matches the token executes its handler; if none
match, the \texttt{otherwise} clause runs. The order of the clauses is
the order of the tests, so a more specific pattern must precede a more
general one that would also match the same input. This is the same
ordering discipline required by any pattern-matching dispatch, and it is
the responsibility of the user, not the engine.

The \texttt{requires}-based detection ensures that the
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
clauses are routed to the \texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
call, while a
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}some\_\allowbreak{}enum\_\allowbreak{}value\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
clause in the same table would be routed to the equality comparison. The
two styles can be mixed in a single table, although doing so is unusual
because the key types would have to agree across clauses. In practice, a
table is either fully value-based or fully pattern-based; the engine
supports both uniformly.

\section{Worked Examples of Pattern
Matching}\label{ch9-worked-examples-of-pattern-matching}

This section presents a series of small examples that illustrate the
matching semantics in isolation. Each example shows a pattern, an input,
and the result, with a brief explanation of why the engine produces that
result. These examples are intended to build a precise intuition for the
engine's behaviour.

\begin{code}
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches("123"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(""));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches("12a"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[0-9]*">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-z]*">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-z]*">::matches("hello"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[a-z]*">::matches("Hello"));
\end{code}

The first assertion holds because \texttt{[0-9]+} requires at least one
digit and the input is all digits. The second fails because \texttt{+}
requires at least one. The third fails because end-anchoring demands
that the entire input be digits, and the trailing \texttt{a} breaks the
match. The fourth and fifth hold because \texttt{*} permits zero
matches. The sixth holds because the input is all lowercase letters. The
seventh fails because the initial uppercase \texttt{H} is not in the
class \texttt{[a-z]}.

\begin{code}
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches("alpha_7"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches("_"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches("7alpha"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<".">::matches("x"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<".">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<".*">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<".*">::matches("anything"));
\end{code}

The identifier pattern matches \texttt{"alpha\_\allowbreak{}7"} because
the first character is a letter and the rest are word characters. It
matches \texttt{"\_\allowbreak{}"} because the first character is an
underscore and the \texttt{*} permits the rest to be empty. It fails to
match \texttt{"7alpha"} because the first character is a digit, which is
not in the initial class. The wildcard examples show that
\texttt{.\allowbreak{}} requires exactly one character and
\texttt{.\allowbreak{}*} accepts any string, including the empty string.

\begin{code}
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"if">::matches("if"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"if">::matches("iffy"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"if">::matches("If"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"^if">::matches("if"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"if$">::matches("if"));
\end{code}

The literal-pattern examples show whole-input matching: \texttt{"if"}
matches \texttt{"if"}, while \texttt{"iffy"} and \texttt{"If"} do not.
The explicit-anchor forms \texttt{\textasciicircum{}if}, \texttt{if\$},
and \texttt{\textasciicircum{}if\$} also match \texttt{"if"}. A trailing
\texttt{\$} requires the input to have ended; it does not require a
dollar-sign byte.

\section{Limitations of the Engine}\label{ch9-limitations-of-the-engine}

The \texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
engine is intentionally limited. This section catalogues the limitations
honestly, so that users can decide whether the engine is appropriate for
a given task. The limitations are not defects; they are design choices
that keep the engine small and predictable. Users who need richer
semantics should use the PEG engine described in Chapters 27 and 28.

The engine does not support alternation. There is no \texttt{|}
operator, and therefore no way to write a single pattern that matches
either \texttt{"if"} or \texttt{"for"}. The workaround is to use
multiple
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiations combined by \texttt{||} at the C++ level, as shown in the
keyword example above. This is slightly more verbose but has the
advantage of making each alternative a distinct type, which can aid
diagnostics.

The engine does not support grouping. There are no parentheses, and
therefore no way to apply a quantifier to a sub-expression of more than
one atom. The pattern \texttt{(\allowbreak{}ab)\allowbreak{}*} is not
expressible. This is a more fundamental restriction than the lack of
alternation, because there is no simple workaround at the C++ level.
Users who need to recognise repeated multi-character sequences must use
the PEG engine.

The engine does not support backreferences. There is no capture, and
therefore no way to refer to a previously matched substring later in the
pattern. This is consistent with the engine's pure-function design: a
match is a function of pattern and input, with no state carried between
calls. Backreferences are incompatible with that design.

The engine does not support shorthand classes. There is no
\texttt{\textbackslash{}d}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}w},
\texttt{\textbackslash{}s}, or any other backslash escape. Every class
must spell out its characters or ranges explicitly. There is also no
escape mechanism for matching special characters literally; the
characters \texttt{.\allowbreak{}}, \texttt{[}, \texttt{]}, \texttt{*},
\texttt{+}, \texttt{\textasciicircum{}}, \texttt{\$}, and \texttt{-}
cannot be matched as literals in positions where the parser would
interpret them as operators. Inside a class, \texttt{-} is the only
character with a special meaning, and only when it appears between two
other characters.

The engine does not support counted repetition. There is no
\texttt{\{n\}}, \texttt{\{n,\allowbreak{}\}}, or
\texttt{\{n,\allowbreak{}m\}} form. A pattern that requires exactly
three digits must be written as \texttt{[0-9][0-9][0-9]}. This is
verbose but straightforward for small counts, and it is adequate for the
lexical-classification tasks the engine is designed for.

The engine does not support the \texttt{?} quantifier. There is no
zero-or-one operator. A pattern that permits an optional character must
be expressed by other means, typically by listing the variants
explicitly or by restructuring the surrounding dispatch. The \texttt{*}
quantifier can sometimes substitute, but it permits more than one
occurrence, which is not always acceptable.

Classes cannot nest. The engine finds the first \texttt{]} after the
opening \texttt{[} and treats everything in between as the class body.
There is no support for \texttt{[[a-z]\&\&[a-m]]} or any other nested
form. The class body is a flat sequence of singletons and ranges. This
is sufficient for the character sets that arise in lexical
classification, but it precludes more sophisticated class expressions.

Contrasting with full POSIX or ECMAScript regex, the
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
engine omits: alternation, grouping, backreferences, shorthand classes,
counted repetition, non-greedy quantifiers, case-insensitive matching,
multiline mode, lookahead, lookbehind, and Unicode character properties.
What it provides is a small, fast, compile-time-evaluable matcher for
anchored full matches over a tiny but useful regex subset. For tasks
within its scope it is adequate and convenient; for tasks beyond its
scope the PEG engine is the recommended alternative.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Choosing Between
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} and
the PEG
Engine}{Choosing Between  and the PEG Engine}}\label{ch9-choosing-between-pattern-and-the-peg-engine}

The library offers two recognition engines: the
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
engine described in this chapter, and the PEG engine described in
Chapters 27 and 28. The two are complementary, and the choice between
them depends on the task.
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
appropriate when the recognition task is a single anchored
classification predicate, when the pattern is short and fixed, and when
the set of categories is small. The PEG engine is appropriate when the
recognition task involves sequencing, alternation, or recursion, when
the grammar is too complex to express as a single regex, or when the
result of recognition is a structured parse tree rather than a boolean.

A useful rule of thumb is to reach for
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
first, and to move to the PEG engine only when
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
proves insufficient. The
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
engine is smaller, simpler, and has no dependencies beyond the header
itself. Its matching algorithm is easy to reason about, and its
limitations are easy to work around for the kinds of tasks it was
designed for. The PEG engine is more powerful but also more complex, and
it brings its own concepts of grammar definition, combinator
composition, and parse-tree construction.

In a typical MuDSL workflow,
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
used in the early stages of a pipeline, where tokens are classified into
kinds, and the PEG engine is used in later stages, where the token
stream is parsed into a tree. The two engines share no code but coexist
peacefully in the same header. A program can use both without conflict,
and the choice of engine for a given task is purely a matter of
expressiveness and convenience.

\section{\texorpdfstring{A Note on \texttt{constexpr}
Evaluability}{A Note on  Evaluability}}\label{ch9-a-note-on-constexpr-evaluability}

Every member of
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{}}, and the helpers it calls are
likewise \texttt{constexpr}. This means that a match can be performed
entirely at compile time, provided the input is a compile-time constant.
The \texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert} examples shown earlier in this
chapter are not merely illustrative; they are genuine compile-time tests
that the compiler evaluates during template instantiation.

Compile-time matching is useful for asserting invariants about literal
strings embedded in a program. For example, a library author might
assert that a particular configuration string matches an expected
pattern, with the check performed at build time and with no run-time
cost. The same machinery can be used in \texttt{consteval} functions to
construct compile-time lookup tables indexed by pattern.

The cost of compile-time matching is borne by the compiler, and for very
long inputs or pathological patterns the compile time can become
noticeable. For typical lexical-classification patterns and short inputs
the cost is negligible. Users who observe slow compiles should check
whether a
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiation is being invoked repeatedly in a tight \texttt{constexpr}
context, and consider hoisting the result into a named
\texttt{constexpr} variable.

At run time, the matcher is a plain function with no virtual dispatch,
no allocation, and no exceptions. It can be called from hot loops
without concern. The recursion depth is bounded by the pattern length,
and the work per recursive call is a small constant. For the inputs the
engine is designed for, performance is not a consideration.

\section{Composing Patterns with
Operators}\label{ch9-composing-patterns-with-operators}

Although
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
itself has no composition operators, the surrounding \texttt{dsl}
namespace provides facilities for combining predicates. Chapter 7 covers
the operators feature in detail, including the predicate-composition
operators that can be used to build compound matchers from
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiations. The typical pattern is to wrap a
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}matches}
call in a predicate and then combine predicates with \texttt{\&\&} or
\texttt{||}.

This style of composition is more verbose than writing a single regex
with alternation, but it has the advantage of preserving the type
identity of each component pattern. Each
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiation remains a distinct type, which can be useful for
diagnostics and for dispatch. The composition happens at the predicate
level, not at the pattern level, and the resulting predicate can be used
anywhere a boolean predicate is accepted.

For the simple classification tasks that are the primary use case of
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
composition is rarely needed. The patterns are usually disjoint, and a
sequence of \texttt{if} tests is the clearest expression of the
classification. Composition becomes more valuable when patterns overlap
in complex ways, or when the same pattern is reused across multiple
classifiers. In those cases the predicate-composition operators provide
a clean way to build reusable building blocks.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Diagnostics and the \texttt{value}
Member}{Diagnostics and the  Member}}\label{ch9-diagnostics-and-the-value-member}

The \texttt{value} member of
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
provided primarily for diagnostics. Because it is a
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view},
it can be included in error messages, log lines, and debug output
without any run-time cost. A typical use is in a fallback handler that
reports which patterns were tried and failed, to assist the user in
understanding why a given input was classified as ``other''.

\begin{code}
auto report = [](std::string_view input) {
  if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(input)) return "number";
  if (dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">::matches(input)) return "word";
  return "other";
};
// In a diagnostics path:
std::cerr << "pattern value: " << dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::value << "\n";
\end{code}

The \texttt{value} member is also useful when constructing dispatch
tables programmatically. A table-builder that accepts
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
instantiations can expose the pattern text to the user for confirmation,
without having to parse the template arguments. This is a small
convenience, but it makes the engine slightly easier to use in
metaprogramming contexts where the pattern text is not otherwise
visible.

Because \texttt{value} is a \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} into the
\texttt{FixedString} NTTP, it is valid for the lifetime of the program.
There is no dangling-pointer risk, no matter how long the
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} is held. This is a consequence of the
NTTP storage model: the pattern text lives in static storage for the
entire program, and the \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view} merely points
into it.

\section{Summary}\label{ch9-summary}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}S\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a small, compile-time-evaluable regex-subset matcher parameterised on
a \texttt{FixedString} NTTP. It exposes a \texttt{value} member for
diagnostics and a \texttt{matches(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member
for testing whether an input satisfies the pattern. Matching is anchored
at both ends: the entire input must be described by the pattern. The
supported syntax comprises the wildcard \texttt{.\allowbreak{}},
character classes \texttt{[abc]}, ranges \texttt{[a-z]} within classes,
the \texttt{*} and \texttt{+} postfix quantifiers, and the
\texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} anchors (which are accepted
but not meaningful). The engine is implemented by three private helpers:
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}class}, which scans a class body for
singletons and ranges; \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}one}, which
dispatches on the atom kind; and \texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}here}, the
recursive matcher that parses atoms, applies quantifiers with greedy
backtracking semantics, and enforces end-anchoring.

Integration with the dispatch machinery of Chapter 8 is structural: any
type exposing a
\texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} bool matches(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view)\allowbreak{}}
member is treated as a pattern key by
\texttt{WhenClause:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}try\_\allowbreak{}invoke},
which converts the runtime key to a \texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}view}
and delegates to the matcher. This lets
\texttt{when\textless{}\allowbreak{}dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
clauses sit alongside value-based clauses in a
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match} table, with the routing
decided at compile time by \texttt{if constexpr}.

The engine is deliberately limited. It omits alternation, grouping,
backreferences, shorthand classes, counted repetition, the \texttt{?}
quantifier, nested classes, and case-insensitive matching. These
omissions keep the engine small and predictable, and they scope it to
the lexical-classification tasks for which it was designed. For richer
recognition, including sequencing, alternation, and recursive grammars,
the PEG engine of Chapters 27 and 28 is the appropriate tool. Within its
scope,
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
provides a fast, allocation-free, \texttt{constexpr}-evaluable matcher
that is convenient to use and easy to reason about.
